/** @mainpage USBRadioPlus developer reference

USBRadioPlus implements the RadioPlus Asterisk channel technology, native radio
signaling, source processing, and the interactive tuning utility. This reference
describes implementation contracts. The installed manual pages describe radio
configuration and operation.

@section architecture Source layout

Transmit observability uses opt-in Asterisk debug level 5 records prefixed
URP_TXTRACE. Request, render, queue, write, and HID-call boundaries are distinct:
generation or enqueue is not DAC playback, and HID return is not RF confirmation.
OSS queue delay uses occupied bytes, including partial fragments. The PortAudio
adapter does not claim an unavailable queue timestamp. See the configuration
manual for trace enablement and disable instructions. No new PTT policy or
audio buffering is introduced by the trace.

- chan_usbradioplus.c adapts OSS/libusb radio devices to Asterisk.
- chan_usbradioplus_modern.c adapts the ASL radio-device API and PortAudio.
- usbradioplus_channel_common.h defines shared channel lifecycle and tuning calls.
- usbradioplus_channel_core.h defines PCM routing, lock-free queues, and echo state.
- usbradioplus_native_tick.c exchanges fixed PCM blocks with the native worker;
  that worker runs the native receive/transmit rendering sequence.
- usbradioplus_processing.h exposes validated profiles and live processing controls.
- txagc_config describes the shared FFmpeg graph. Filtering, emphasis,
  equalization, dynamics, and limiting are implemented by that graph.
- usbradioplus_radio.h contains carrier, CTCSS, DCS, measurement, and
  transmit-signaling state.
- usbradioplus_ctcss.h, usbradioplus_dcs.h, and usbradioplus_hardware.h provide
  tone/code generation and parallel-port radio programming.
- scripts/usbradioplus-tune implements the screen-reader-friendly tuner.
- tests and tests_py contain executable unit, failure-injection, integration,
  configuration, utility, and install checks. Upstream reference fixtures are test data.

@section units Audio buffers and units

The CM119 path runs at 48,000 samples per second with 960 samples in a 20 ms
mono block. App_rpt starts at 8,000 samples per second; each channel stores its
rate explicitly. Equal input and output rates bypass sample-rate conversion.
Signed PCM spans -32768 through 32767. Native floating-point workspaces retain
PCM amplitude units. FFmpeg format conversion supplies the graph's normalized
audio representation. dBFS measurements use the full signed PCM range.

The app_rpt-to-CM119 program ring is the only cross-clock audio queue between
app_rpt and the hardware path. It accepts PCM at the active app_rpt rate and
renders from its first available sample; it has no source-audio startup fill or
playback reserve. Its 110 ms occupancy target is a clock-recovery setpoint, not
a fixed latency or keyed-transmit admission delay. Its one persistent
libsamplerate stream both converts that source rate to 48 kHz and gently adjusts
the conversion ratio for clock drift. This correction is independent of device
and network jitter buffers and changes no PTT state. A temporary program-source
shortfall is concealed smoothly by the renderer rather than rendered as an
abrupt silence insertion.

Both adapters admit app_rpt program frames continuously, including idle
silence, except while echo playback owns the input. PTT transitions preserve
the program ring and resampler state. Native-rate input uses the same stream
with a nominal one-to-one ratio. This continuous PCM flow affects audio only:
app_rpt and tuning retain ownership of PTT, and ring occupancy does not extend
it.

The hardware callback and the per-channel native worker exchange fixed,
preallocated PCM blocks through bounded SPSC queues. The callback is the input
producer and rendered-output consumer; the worker is the inverse endpoint and
alone owns FFmpeg, RNNoise, and native sample-rate conversion. These queues do
not replace or add an app_rpt bridge. If no completed worker block is available,
the callback writes one silent block and retains its normal cadence. A worker
shortfall therefore cannot change PTT, COS, CTCSS, or DCS state.

The device writer submits one 48 kHz stereo block per completed capture
interval, including while idle. COS and CTCSS decode do not control device
write cadence. The OSS writer does not prepend silence when its queue empties.
Its queue-depth bound and short/failed-write diagnostics still apply during
idle operation. PortAudio retains the ASL audio API's device recovery.

When the local chain is enabled, its enabled RNNoise and FFmpeg dynamics
stages advance every native interval with receive cpu_saver_enabled=no.
RX CPU saver bypasses those stages while the receiver is unqualified. Existing
receive qualification, repeat routing, and final transmit muting still
control audibility; processing continuity does not key the transmitter.

Hardware mixer settings use 0 through 999. Hardware gain is expressed in dB
relative to setting 500. Receiver CTCSS decoder gain affects the detector input;
it does not change voice gain. Native CTCSS is generated at the direct
`[ctcss] transmit_peak_dbfs` PCM peak and mixed after transmitter dynamics and
limiting. Hardware output gain then sets the DAC's analog level. Saturating PCM
conversion prevents signed wraparound.
The transmit preemphasis_enabled option controls only the composite graph's
pre-emphasis stage. Final limiting uses the voice/telemetry profile's explicit
lookahead-limiter controls independently of pre-emphasis.

Native DCS uses its own direct `[dcs] peak_dbfs` PCM peak and a fixed shared-FFmpeg
spectral-shaping graph. It bypasses CTCSS generation, transmitter pre-emphasis,
and speech processing, while sharing only the configured hardware output route
and downstream hardware gain.

Each direction declares exactly one signaling method in its `[receive]` or
`[transmit]` profile. Selecting receive CTCSS requires a non-disabled CTCSS
source and a supported receive-tone list; selecting transmit CTCSS requires
one supported default transmit tone. A receive-to-transmit map is required
only when both directions select CTCSS; then both supported-tone lists must
have equal length. Selecting DCS requires the corresponding DCS code in
`000N`- through `777I`-style spelling
(three octal digits followed by `N` or `I`). The
configuration parser rejects
an incomplete or invalid selected method before a channel can start, rather
than allowing the radio engine to silently fall back to carrier behavior.
For a configured profile, omitted clean-slate `[receive]`, `[transmit]`,
`[ctcss]`, and `[dcs]` options resolve to the concrete values in the shipped
sample. Explicit scoped values take precedence over flat-section values.
Integer signaling controls validate the exact storage units used by the channel
adapter: receive squelch delay is 0--511 ms, receive-on and transmit-off delay
are 0--3000 20 ms frames, receive CTCSS relax is 0 or 1, and transmit/receive
frequencies are nonnegative integral hertz. This keeps a successful reload
from deferring a malformed numeric value to channel construction.

@section concurrency Thread and resource ownership

Profile snapshot getters clear their output when a radio profile is absent.
Workers must never consume uninitialized gain, routing, or CTCSS data while a
profile is unavailable; the missing-profile status remains available to callers.

Each channel owns its native graphs, detector, program ring, and a native
worker. The worker exclusively owns mutable resamplers, RNNoise state, echo
recording, graph execution, and worker measurements. The hardware callback
only snapshots signaling state, exchanges bounded PCM through SPSC queues, and
publishes the immediate PTT request. Worker startup occurs only after graph
preparation; shutdown joins the worker and releases queued graph references
before graphs or channel resources are destroyed. The processing settings mutex
protects profile snapshots. Never hold it while looking up or locking an
Asterisk channel: app_rpt can read processing settings while holding channel
locks. The scanner copies the needed profile data before channel lookup.

The native radio callback, including its carrier and CTCSS detector stages,
uses only preallocated caller-owned state. It must not call Asterisk logging,
take locks, allocate memory, perform file I/O, or execute FFmpeg, RNNoise, or
sample-rate conversion. Configuration parsing, construction, destruction, and
native rendering are outside that callback and retain their useful diagnostics.
PTT is requested synchronously from the current signaling-engine state at the
callback boundary; worker output, worker queue occupancy, program-ring
occupancy, and audio shortfalls must never key or unkey the transmitter.

Native worker measurements are published as coherent lock-free snapshots.
Control-plane meters and reset requests must use that snapshot/request boundary
instead of reading or changing worker-owned graph, RNNoise, SRC, or echo state
directly. A reader receives the most recently published complete snapshot, or
reports it unavailable after bounded retries rather than waiting for rendering;
a pinned pair of snapshot buffers can therefore leave measurements briefly stale.

Configured radio traces remain preallocated in-memory diagnostic buffers. The
obsolete receive/transmit capture toggles and file-capture path are absent, so
they cannot add callback I/O or an alternate real-time path.

Each incoming-link audiohook owns a single link-render worker through its
Asterisk datastore. The callback is the producer of a preallocated input SPSC
PCM queue and the consumer of its paired output queue; the worker owns FFmpeg
execution and graph-meter updates. At each callback the output write cursor is
snapshotted before current input is queued, so only a complete earlier block
can be rendered. This gives deterministic one-frame processing chronology,
without a callback lock, allocation, or FFmpeg call.

Initial output, a late worker result, malformed metadata, and output from an
older reload generation are concealed with a short taper to silence. The
published link-worker snapshot records queue overflows, concealed underflows,
malformed output, graph shortfalls, and input/output levels. CLI readers pin a
published snapshot buffer and can receive a briefly stale or unavailable
snapshot rather than waiting for the worker. Link processing preserves the
attached channel's negotiated sample rate and has no PTT policy.

Hook teardown first detaches the audiohook, then stops and joins its worker
before graph slots, queued graph references, or queue storage are destroyed.
Reload generation tags prevent old queued audio from replaying after a new
graph becomes active. Hardware adapters own their device references and
coordinate audio/HID workers when swapping USB assignments.

@section verification Building and checking the reference

Run `make docs` with Doxygen 1.9.8 or newer, Graphviz, and Python 3 installed.
Generated HTML is written to `build/doxygen/html`; XML is written beside it. Doxygen checks
undocumented symbols and enum values, parameter and return documentation, and
invalid markup. Every diagnostic fails the target.

The required quality workflow runs Doxygen concurrently with lint and static
analysis. All three results must succeed before the Debian 12/13 amd64/arm64
test matrix. Release callers depend on that same required quality workflow.
Only successful main-branch checks publish this reference to GitHub Pages.

Shell documentation uses double-hash comments. The documentation filter exposes
actual shell function names and those comments to Doxygen without executing
shell code or supplying missing documentation. Python uses standard docstrings
with Doxygen commands for parameters and return values.
*/
